# Erdős Problem #99 — fixed affine-lattice bulk exclusion

## Theorem

Let \(u,v\) be linearly independent vectors and let
\[
\Lambda=\mathbb Zu+\mathbb Zv,
\qquad
a_\Lambda=|\det(u,v)|.
\]
Put
\[
R_\Lambda=\frac12\max\{|u+v|,\ |u-v|\}.
\]
For every finite \(n\)-point subset \(P\subset\Lambda\), let
\(K=\operatorname{conv}P\) and \(D_P=\operatorname{diam}P\). Then
\[
n a_\Lambda
\le \operatorname{area}(K+B(0,R_\Lambda))
\le \frac{\pi}{4}(D_P+2R_\Lambda)^2.
\tag{A.1}
\]
Consequently, if \(P\) is a global diameter minimizer, the strict triangular-lattice
comparison
\[
D_P^2=D(n)^2<c n,
\qquad
c=\frac{2\sqrt3}{\pi},
\]
gives
\[
\boxed{
a_\Lambda-\frac{\sqrt3}{2}
<
\pi R_\Lambda\sqrt{\frac{c}{n}}
\;+\frac{\pi R_\Lambda^2}{n}.
}
\tag{A.2}
\]
In particular, for every fixed lattice with
\[
a_\Lambda>\frac{\sqrt3}{2},
\]
there is an \(n_0(\Lambda)\) such that no \(n\)-point subset of that lattice is a
global minimizer for \(n\ge n_0(\Lambda)\).

## Proof

Use the half-open centered fundamental parallelogram
\[
F=\{su+tv:-\tfrac12\le s,t<\tfrac12\}.
\]
Its translates by \(\Lambda\) have disjoint interiors and each has area
\(a_\Lambda\). The norm is convex as a function of \((s,t)\), so its maximum on the
closed coefficient square is attained at a corner. Hence
\[
F\subseteq B(0,R_\Lambda).
\]
Since \(p\in K\) for every \(p\in P\), the disjoint cells \(p+F\) lie in
\(K+B(0,R_\Lambda)\). This proves the first inequality in (A.1). The Minkowski sum
has diameter at most \(D_P+2R_\Lambda\), so the isodiametric inequality proves the
second.

If \(P\) is globally optimal, \(D_P^2<c n\). Substituting this into (A.1) gives
\[
n a_\Lambda
<
\frac{\pi}{4}(\sqrt{c n}+2R_\Lambda)^2
=\frac{\sqrt3}{2}n+\pi R_\Lambda\sqrt{c n}+\pi R_\Lambda^2,
\]
which is (A.2). The fixed-lattice exclusion follows because the right side of
(A.2) tends to zero after division by \(n\). \(\square\)

## Relation to the current frontier

The theorem includes the fixed-angle oblique-lattice exclusion as the special case
\(|u|=|v|=1\), where \(a_\Lambda=\sin\theta\). It also covers fixed anisotropic
affine images not represented by a single rhombus angle.

It does not exclude a sequence of affine lattices whose covolume approaches
\(\sqrt3/2\) at the boundary scale \(O(n^{-1/2})\). This is exactly the scale of the
distributed-strain regression: fixed affine bulk is eliminated, but vanishing strain
can still pay only \(O(\sqrt n)\) total area. Nor does the theorem control a
nonlattice rhombic mesh or the exterior constraints of a finite monomer domain.

Thus this is a genuine quantitative narrowing, not a Level I solution. The remaining
Target A bridge is still the source-Hall/Schur response inequality, or a finite
boundary-compatible concentration theorem for the surviving vanishing-strain branch.
